TURTLE VILLAGE REFLECTION:
One place I used a while loop for was in the functions that took in user input. This is because the purpose
of those functions such as ask_choice_str() and ask_choice_int() is to keep on taking in user inputs until
the user enters a valid input. A while loop is the best fit for this because while loops keep repeating until
a certain condition is met. When it came to drawing the roads, horizontally and vertically, for loops were used
because the process of drawing the lines had to be done a fixed amount of times in order to get the right amount.
A for loop is used when you want an action to be repeated a specific amount of times. I used try/except for the
ask_choice_int function when the program converts the user input into type int because if the user doesn't enter
an integer, it will cause an error when trying to convert something that is not an integer into type int. I guarded
against a value error that can cause my program to crash if handled incorrectly. When it came to line spacing and
line length limits, I decided to break pep 8 rules for the sake of greater clarity and readability in my code. I 
like to use extra blank lines between functions and separate blocks in order to differentiate between them. I also
like to use long inline comments because if I break them up, the reader will have a hard time reading them.
